<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Gurma people</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Gourmantché, Gurmanche, Gulmance</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Gurma, or Gourmantché, are a Gur-speaking people of eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burkina-faso">Burkina Faso</a> and neighbouring parts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/niger-river">Niger</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/togo">Togo</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benin-empire">Benin</a>. Their traditional kingdoms, centred on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fada-ngourma">Fada N'Gourma</a>, were among the Mossi-related states of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/volta-river">Volta</a> basin. Before the 19th century Gurma chiefs ruled Liptako, until Fulbe herders rose against them and founded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emirate">Emirate</a> of Liptako around 1810.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/gurma-people.jpg" alt="Gurma people" width="320" height="427" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Ji-Elle · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Panier_semi-sph%C3%A9rique-Gourmantch%C3%A9-_Mus%C3%A9e_africain_de_Lyon.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
